#
# Shared ESP-IDF seed configuration for the `framework = arduino, espidf`
# (hybrid) environments.
#
# SEEDED FROM:
#   ~/.platformio/packages/framework-arduinoespressif32-libs/esp32/sdkconfig
#   package esp32-core-3.3.11-libs (arduino-esp32 3.3.11)
#   (ESP-IDF 5.5.5, platform-espressif32 55.03.311)
#
# This is the same file pioarduino's own write_sdkconfig_file() copies for the
# prebuilt path (espidf.py:712); copying it verbatim is what makes the hybrid
# build bit-compatible with the prebuilt Arduino libraries. The Arduino core
# hard-fails at CMake time without it (it requires CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ=1000,
# while bare IDF defaults to 100), so this file is a hard requirement, not a
# tuning knob.
#
# CHANGED VS THE SEED: nothing inline. The only deltas are appended at the end
# of this file, under the "PROJECT OVERRIDES" banner — keep it that way so a
# re-seed from a newer package is a plain overwrite-then-reappend.
#
# HAZARD — READ BEFORE EDITING:
#   sdkconfig.defaults only SEEDS the configuration. Once the resolved
#   sdkconfig.<env> file exists it is the authority and this file is ignored.
#   After editing this file (or sdkconfig.defaults.tls) you MUST delete the
#   stale generated sdkconfig.<env> (e.g. sdkconfig.openevse_wifi_tft_v1) or
#   run `pio run -t fullclean`, otherwise the change silently does nothing and
#   the firmware is byte-identical. The generated sdkconfig.<env> files are
#   gitignored; only the seeds are committed.
#
#   Note also that PlatformIO's cmake-reconfigure check only stats
#   sdkconfig.defaults, not the overlay files — another reason to delete the
#   generated sdkconfig.<env> by hand after any seed edit.
#
#
# Automatically generated file. DO NOT EDIT.
# Espressif IoT Development Framework (ESP-IDF) 5.5.5 Project Configuration
#
